            <ArticleTitle>Disseminated Mycobacterium chimaera Presenting as Vertebral Osteomyelitis.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>9893743</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1155/2017/9893743</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Mycobacterium chimaera, a member of the Mycobacterium avium complex, is a slow-growing, nontuberculous mycobacterium associated with outbreaks in cardiac-surgery patients supported on heart-lung machines. We report a case of an elderly woman on chronic prednisone who presented with a six-month history of worsening chronic back pain, recurrent low-grade fevers, and weight loss. Imaging identified multilevel vertebral osteomyelitis and lumbar soft-tissue abscess. Abscess culture identified M. chimaera.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
